+## 3. Why the autofix job cannot pass (structural chain, from ci.yml read verbatim at HEAD `32dccbb`)
+
+1. **The analyzer step cannot fail by construction** — both pyerrorfix invocations run under an or-chain fallback that discards their exit status, and a later guard overwrites the SARIF output with a valid empty skeleton whenever the file is empty or unparseable. Step exit status is 0 by construction.
+2. **The analyzer is a local package and does resolve** — core ships `pyerrorfix/__init__.py` (120 B), `pyerrorfix/__main__.py` (89 B → `pyerrorfix.cli.main`), `pyerrorfix/cli.py` (4,536 B); `PYTHONPATH=".:core"` is exported in-step. It is NOT declared in `requirements.txt` (39 B) or `pyproject.toml` — by design (local engine), not a missing dependency.
+3. **The only unarmored step is `github/codeql-action/upload-sarif@v4`** with `if: always()` and no `continue-on-error` — it runs unconditionally on every scheduled execution.
+4. **core is a private repository** — `raw.githubusercontent.com/Supremeai-bd/supremeai-core/main/requirements.txt` returns 404 unauthenticated (public repos serve raw 200). SARIF upload to the code-scanning API on a private repo requires GitHub Advanced Security; without it the upload fails deterministically. Four consecutive nightly failures on four different commits (`419a838`/`feda45e`×2/`32dccbb`) — while push runs on overlapping days are green — is consistent with a structural failure and inconsistent with any code-dependent cause.
+5. Run 42 completed in ~103 s (08:09:25Z → 08:11:08Z): the failing job's log tail (via `github.get_failed_logs`) shows the codeql-action POST phase ("Uploading combined SARIF debug artifact" → cleanup). **Honest limitation**: the tool returns a trailing snippet only; the exact API error text of the upload step was not captured. The step identity follows by elimination — steps 1–2 (checkout/setup-python) are shared-technology steps that do not fail 4/4 nights on schedule only, and step 3 cannot exit non-zero (see 3.1).
+
+## 4. Consequences on the board
+
+- **P0 noise, nightly**: each night's red run re-fires the in-workflow federation watchdog (`federation_ci_failed` dispatch, `|| true`-armored) → a fresh P0-critical incident row with a NEW run id. #3050's demotion can never trigger because each incident references the then-newest run (zero newer runs of any kind). Forecast: next red ≈ 2026-10-31 in the observed 07:2x–08:1xZ start cluster (nominal cron `0 2 * * *`; observed starts run ~6 h late — GitHub schedule-delay window, recorded as observation, cause not determined).
+- **Filing-gap honesty**: the open-repo receiver (`pipeline.yml` `federation_ci_failed` handler) went live 2026-10-27T10:00Z (#749/#761, commit `a31ad8b`). Nightly failures of runs 40 (10-28 07:58Z) and 41 (10-29 08:06Z) produced **no surviving incident rows** (open-repo issue search "ci failed on main", any state, probed 08:2xZ: only #3100). Cause of the gap not determined within envelope (silent `|| true` dispatch failure, receiver filter, or token absence on those nights).
+- The `test` job's own watchdog step is inert on schedule (job skipped) — no test-suite signal is lost; nothing about main's health is actually red.
+
+## 5. Remedy options (all outside the agent envelope — satellite repo + `.github/` protected path)
+
+| Option | Change (one line each) | Owner lane |
+|---|---|---|
+| A (minimal, recommended) | Add `continue-on-error: true` to the `Upload SARIF` step in core's `.github/workflows/ci.yml` — sidecar degrades to advisory, nightly reds stop | core-repo admin/committer |
+| B (equivalent) | Gate the step off for private/no-AS repos, or replace code-scanning upload with a plain `actions/upload-artifact` | core-repo admin/committer |
+| C (platform) | Enable GitHub Advanced Security on core (paid) so the upload succeeds | admin/billing |
+| D (watchdog hygiene) | Receiver-side: ignore `autofix`-job failures or dedupe by failing-step signature (extends #3050 family) | open-repo `.github/` (admin) |
